recently there was an article in the  financial times the financial times was  talking all about the epic historic bull  market investor psychology and risks and  potential sell-offs they were making the  argument that actually we've only lived  in a bull market and that there is tons  and tons of risk that assets could turn  over very quickly there's a number of  investors that also started to tweet and  be like look all the young people  they've never lived during a market  downturn they continue to say that they  don't understand how to underwrite risk  they don't understand how to weather  that asset sell-off they're all pointing  to last friday as that sell-off that  made them think about it so i went and i  did some analysis and what i did is i  looked at people who were graduating in  2009. if you graduated college in 2009  you're approximately 35 years old today  now if you look at that group of folks  what you find is that they have actually  in my opinion lived through more  financial duress than any other  generation in history what do i mean  well if you graduated in 2009 you walked  into one of the worst job markets in  history literally there was people  losing their jobs left and right no one  was hiring a lot of those folks had to  move in with their parents on top of  that you saw family and friends all  losing their jobs getting completely  wiped out financially there was mass  chaos it was not called the great  financial crisis for any reason now 2009  was not the only thing it took until  march of 2010 for people to actually  start to get private job growth so for  an entire year people who are graduating  in 2009 were sitting on the sidelines  could not find jobs it was very very  difficult you can see here that it  wasn't until 2010 when people actually  started hiring again now that would be  bad enough if you started your career  during a global financial crisis but  then of course if you think of last year  march of 2020 asset prices were selling  off due to the covid concerns the  uncertainty the fear the doubt that was  in the market we saw gold go down over  10 we saw stocks go down 25 to 35 and we  saw bitcoin go down 50 in a single day  it was a recession there was two  consecutive quarters of negative growth  so quite literally people who graduated  in 2009 have lived through two separate  recessions in a 12-year period on top of  that last year there was over 6.5  million people a week that were losing  their jobs unemployment reached almost  15 percent we watched people have to run  businesses or live and work at  businesses that could not operate this  was the hardest business environment in  the last 50 years the government forced  you to go home and run your company  while you sat at home locked away  without your freedom to go to your place  of business and operate as you had been  so not only did those people live  through the global financial crisis not  only did they live through the 2020  recession and the lockdowns but on top  of that most young people have actually  invested in bitcoin and cryptocurrencies  and if you look at that since 2011 there  have been three times over 80 percent  drawdowns in the price of bitcoin  since graduation people who are about 35  years old have lived through three  separate 80 plus percent drawdowns but  let's give people the benefit of the  doubt let's say that most people were  not invested in the market until 2016 or  2017. if you were invested in 2017 in  2018 a year later you lived through an  80 plus percent drawdown in the price of  bitcoin other assets outside of bitcoin  in the crypto market drew down 95 to 99  there has never been a time in the  entire stock market that stock investors  or boomers or people older generations  had to live through at 85 to 99  drawdown in assets the single largest  most severe bear market that anyone in  the legacy market would have ever faced  but it's not just about that 2018 bear  market let's say that you weren't even  invested then and you just invested at  the beginning of 2021. if you're a young  person and you invested at the beginning  of 2021 there have been six separate  times this year just in 2021 that you  have weathered a 20 plus percent  drawdown in the price of bitcoin  including a 37 drawdown in september and  more than 50 percent dry down in may to  july so the whole idea that young  investors haven't lived through bear  markets they don't know what a market  downturn looks like is completely wrong  in fact the truth is that the young  investors they have diamond hands  eighty-seven percent of people who owned  bitcoin was seventeen thousand dollars  in 2017 held it all the way down to  three thousand dollars they were buying  more young investors are built different  they're digital native they understand  volatility most of them have a high  degree of their net worth tied up in  assets like bitcoin and cryptocurrencies  that have 80 volatility  they were built for this environment  the older generations the legacy  investors the stock market investors  they can't handle this volatility young  people were built for it they were built  in it and they are best prepared to  continue to invest in this asset so not  only have young people lived through  multiple bear markets and multiple  market downturns in the last 12 years  but on top of that they are used to much  more volatility they are used to much  bigger drawdowns there's not a single  investor in the stock market that  wouldn't run and cry to the government  and ask for a bailout ask for monetary  or fiscal stimulus if the stock market  went down 54  over three months if it went down six  times in a year over 20 percent they  literally would be crying foul they'd be  asking for more circuit breakers they'd  be saying something is broken in the  market they'd be asking for the federal  reserve chairman to be fired they can't  handle the volatility that young people  can young people understand the world  has changed but older people will  continue to point out the young people  and say they don't know what they're  doing they're completely confused

